  "remedy_contracts": [
    "Every acquired upgrade fixture reaches explicit finish after a captured client setup/request/body Result; original drop-before-finish flow retained.",
    "Original primary error remains the aggregate source, completed peer/fixture failures remain separate concrete cleanup objects.",
    "Plaintext primary failure invokes owned abort_and_join; actual completed task abort is a no-op in pinned Tokio state, preserving its typed output.",
    "Pinned finish_after owns the actual AlternativePeer and operation value; successful client or client+proxy stays alive through normal finish, error closes endpoint/aborts/joins under existing bounds.",
    "Done sender release is outside the operation Result block, so ordinary failure also releases the barrier; unrelated observed errors remain failures.",
    "Ordinary plaintext client construction precedes peer spawning, preventing fallible client setup from bypassing already-acquired server cleanup.",
    "Three independent persistence paragraph boundaries repaired without new behavior controls."
  ],
  "limitations": [
    "No author Cargo/compiler/Clippy/runtime verification. Root owns native full selections and final integration gates.",
    "Existing Drop requests abort only; no synchronous join, immediate socket release or permanent OS leak claim.",
    "Outer test deadline, panic or forced cancellation can cancel explicit cleanup; owned Drop fallback persists, without unconditional joined teardown.",
    "Only actual completed peer failures are retained; requested cancellation may prevent unfinished work from ever producing an error.",
    "Nested FixtureFailures exposes primary through source and retains cleanup through concrete field/Display; source traversal alone does not enumerate all secondaries.",
    "Existing peer finish APIs, controls and support source were not modified. Supporting dependency/helper reads remain partial.",
    "This is test fixture error/ownership remediation, not production route, protocol, public API, browser parity or complete architecture audit approval."
  ],
